    </expertiseList>
    <grid>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "name": "1 column layout",
  "sections": [
    {
      "grid": "12",
      "rows": [
        {
          "name": "Article Full",
          "id": "309e2d4b-2d7d-4f87-ba89-b73f65ff57bf",
          "areas": [
            {
              "grid": "12",
              "controls": [
                {
                  "value": "<p>Laboratory accreditation is the best assurance that the data you are relying on was generated in a competent laboratory and strongest protection in the event of disputes or grievances.</p>",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "rte",
                    "view": "rte"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": {
                    "focalPoint": {
                      "left": 0.5,
                      "top": 0.5
                    },
                    "id": 1724,
                    "udi": "umb://media/92b4c4eb4e6b44c5b41b61d22945f96d",
                    "image": "/media/3xcljetl/20190812104839_img_0088.jpg"
                  },
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "media",
                    "view": "media"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "What Is Laboratory Accreditation?",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "headline",
                    "view": "textstring"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "<p>Specifications in concrete quality control often state that tests must be carried out by “an approved laboratory”. The next question is “by whom, and against which standards?”</p>\n<p>To become accredited a laboratory must apply to SANAS. The application must be able to demonstrate that the laboratory’s operations substantially comply with the standard’s requirements.</p>\n<p>Approval is based on previous experience with a testing facility and formal auditing of the facility. The current standard code of practice is entitled “General requirements for the competence of testing and calibration laboratories” or SABS ISO/IEC 17025.</p>\n<p>If the review is successful, an initial audit is scheduled. There are three possible outcomes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure</li>\n<li>Conditional approval subject to clearing of findings (with or without a follow-up audit)</li>\n<li>Unconditional approval</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The accreditation period is five years. Once laboratory accreditation is granted, the laboratory is re-audited after 6 months, then 12 months later, then 18 months after that.</p>",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "rte",
                    "view": "rte"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "<p>To become accredited a laboratory must apply to SANAS. The application must be able to demonstrate that the laboratory’s operations substantially comply with the standard’s requirements.</p>\n<p>This includes showing that it has a track record of producing reliable results, that staff members are competent and that procedures, work instructions, test methods, work sheets, sample reports are all present.</p>\n<p>If the review is successful, an initial audit is scheduled. There are three possible outcomes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure</li>\n<li>Conditional approval subject to clearing of findings (with or without a follow-up audit)</li>\n<li>Unconditional approval</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The accreditation period is five years. Once laboratory accreditation is granted, the laboratory is re-audited after 6 months, then 12 months later, then 18 months after that.</p>",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "rte",
                    "view": "rte"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "Why Use An Accredited Laboratory?",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "headline",
                    "view": "textstring"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "<p><em>For the laboratory</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The management and technical competence of the laboratory is shown to be of the highest international standard.</li>\n<li>Certification of a laboratory to ISO 9001 or ISO 9002 does not of itself demonstrate the competence of a laboratory to produce technically valid data and result.</li>\n<li>It becomes easy to manage the laboratory because procedures and methods are well structured.</li>\n<li>It becomes easier to detect and correct non-conformances (which still may happen)</li>\n<li>It commits senior management to the quality system.</li>\n<li>It ensures independence of the laboratory from other company influences, for example: sales, distribution, finance, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>For the client</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The knowledge that results are traceable to international standards</li>\n<li>The knowledge that tests have been carried out by competent staff on well-maintained, regularly calibrated equipment</li>\n<li>The knowledge that results are as reliable as it possible</li>\n<li>In the event of a dispute the results will carry far more weight than results from a non-accredited laboratory.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is important for the client to realise that most accredited laboratories carry out both accredited and non-accredited work. For example, a laboratory may be accredited for concrete quality control and be asked to test concrete cubes, but not flakiness index - although it may carry out that test. It must be clearly indicated on test reports whether the test results were derived from accredited tests or not.</p>",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "rte",
                    "view": "rte"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "Find Out More About Laboratory Accreditation",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "headline",
                    "view": "textstring"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                },
                {
                  "value": "<p>To find out more about laboratory accreditation and SANAS, visit the SANAS website at <a href=\"http://www.sanas.co.za\">www.sanas.co.za</a>. The site contains all of the relevant documentation and a list of accredited testing and calibration laboratories.</p>\n<p>Roadlab has7 SANAS accredited testing laboratories Germiston, Durban, Belville, Mossel Bay, East London, Bloemfontein and Centurion. They all carry out soil, asphalt, concrete, aggregate and and chemical testing.</p>",
                  "editor": {
                    "alias": "rte",
                    "view": "rte"
                  },
                  "styles": null,
                  "config": null
                }
              ],
              "styles": null,
              "config": null
            }
          ],
          "styles": null,
          "config": null
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}]]></Value>
